Few-shot industrial anomaly detectors that utilize frozen foundation features often achieve strong anomaly rankings, but their thresholds may not maintain consistent false-alarm rates across different categories or under input distribution shifts. To address this gap, we introduce an auditable reliability framework with two complementary strengths: it exposes the exact resolution limit of target-only alarms and tests whether source-category evidence is sufficient for transferable certification, rather than presuming that source pooling ensures control. The framework integrates a low-storage DINOv2 PCA residual with label-free leave-one-image-out (LOIO) p-values derived from $k$ normal supports. Evaluation across 15 MVTec and 12 VisA categories under four corruption types reveals a resolution floor: target-only alarms cannot trigger below $\alpha=1/(k{+}1)$. Empirical normal p-values are observed to be conservative on VisA at $k=4$ and anti-conservative on corrupted MVTec. Additionally, Cross-category Reliability Estimation with Source Support (CRESS) is introduced as a source-assisted procedure that distinguishes reference, threshold-proposal, and certification categories. Across a frozen grid of four $k$ values, five seeds, five conditions, within-dataset tests, and MVTec-to-VisA/MPDD transfer, strict category-level certification consistently returns the fail-closed zero threshold in all 960 gate cells. This result is not solely attributable to Hoeffding looseness: when all observed category losses are zero, even an optimistic, multiplicity-free, deterministic, uniformly valid, distribution-free 95\% upper bound requires at least 14 independent categories to certify $\alpha=0.20$. The frozen protocol requires more, whereas only three or four certification categories are available. Fixed-archive image certificates select positive thresholds in 37\% to 60\% of settings, but these estimate image-level risk under the observed source mixture rather than for new-category risk. Historical non-independent CRESS results are therefore retained solely as empirical diagnostics. Collectively, these findings establish a clear feasibility boundary: the framework distinguishes empirically useful source thresholds from transferable certificates and prevents unsupported claims of target-category control.
